<body>

<h1>AI Internal Linking</h1>
<p class="sub">How it decides which page should link to which, and using which words. Written to be readable without any technical background. Version 0.20.0.</p>

<h2>First, the six words this document needs</h2>

<p>Every field has its own vocabulary, and most of it is unnecessary. These six are genuinely needed, so here they are in plain English. Nothing else in this document assumes you know anything.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Word</th><th>What it actually means</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shortlist</strong></td><td>A small group of pages worth thinking about. Your site might have 350 pages, but only a dozen or two of them could sensibly be linked from any given article. That smaller group is the shortlist. Working it out first is what stops the plugin comparing every page against every other page.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Possible destination</strong></td><td>One page on that shortlist. A page that <em>might</em> end up being linked to, once the wording has been checked.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wording</strong></td><td>The words that become clickable. If a sentence reads "your content marketing strategy should be planned", the wording might be "content marketing strategy". Often called anchor text.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>A number from 0 to 1 answering one question: should these two pages be connected at all? Higher is better.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Naturalness</strong></td><td>A number from 0 to 1 answering a different question: does this particular wording read well where it sits? Higher is better.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Striking distance</strong></td><td>Ranking between 5th and 20th on Google. Close enough that a page is being taken seriously, far enough that there is real room to climb. This is where an internal link helps most.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>One more, only if you use the AI engine.</strong> A <strong>token</strong> is roughly three quarters of a word. AI providers bill by the token, so a 1,000 word page costs about 1,300 tokens to send. That is the only reason the word appears in this document at all.</div>

<h2>The short version</h2>

<p>For every page on your site, the plugin asks three separate questions, in order. Each one can propose links until the page's link budget is used up.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Order</th><th>The question</th><th>Answered using</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Does this page mention a phrase that another of my pages already ranks for on Google, without linking to it?</td><td>Google Search Console</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Reading this page, which of these related pages would an editor link to?</td><td>An AI model, using your own key</td><td>Your API key</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Which pages talk about the same things as this one?</td><td>Word comparison, done on your own server</td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<p>They run in that order because the evidence gets weaker as you go down. What people actually search for beats what a model thinks, and what a model thinks beats counting shared words. The last one runs on every page regardless, so you always get suggestions even with no key.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>The rule that governs all three:</strong> the plugin can only link words that are already written on your page. It never writes a new sentence, never rephrases you, and never adds a link without you approving it.</div>

<h2>The AI Suggestion engine</h2>

<p class="sub">Optional. Needs an API key, which is yours and stays yours. Runs second of the three, after GSC keyword.</p>

<h3>What the model is given</h3>

<p>By the time the model is asked anything, your site has already been read in full. Indexing works through every page, reads its content, and records the words each one uses. That happens once, before any of this, and it is what the rest of the work is built on.</p>

<p>Those records are then used to narrow the choice. This page is compared against every other page you have, and only the closest are kept. The model receives your page's own text together with that shortlist, so it is reading your actual content, not guessing from titles alone:</p>

<pre class="formula">The shortlist   the closest matches for this page,
                minus the page itself,
                minus anything it already links to,
                minus anything already suggested before.

Sent to the model   a numbered list of what survives.
                    How many is your setting: 15 by default,
                    anywhere from 5 to 200.

Each entry is   the page's title, followed by a short summary of
                what that page covers. How long is your setting:
                40 words by default, from 25 up to 200. You can
                switch this to a list of key words, or to the
                title alone, on the Setup screen.

Also sent   your page's title, and the opening part of your page
            (how much is your setting: 1,000 words by default,
             up to 3,000 from the menu, or any number up to 20,000
             if you choose Custom).

Reply limit   short, roughly 600 words, which is plenty for a list of links.
Creativity    turned off completely, so the same page gives the same answer.</pre>

<h3>Where the shortlist comes from, and what never makes it</h3>

<p>The shortlist is not the AI's work. It is produced by the free engine, the same one described under Engine 3 further down, and it runs before the model is involved at all. So the honest sequence is: the free engine decides which pages are even worth considering, and the model only chooses among those.</p>

<p>Getting onto that shortlist takes two things. A page has to be eligible, and then it has to score well enough. Eligibility comes first, and it is absolute.</p>

<h4>Seven ways a page is ruled out before any comparison happens</h4>

<table>
<tr><th>A page is never considered if</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>It is the page being read</td><td>Nothing links to itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is not published</td><td>Drafts, private and pending pages are not link destinations</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is not one of the content types you ticked as link targets</td><td>Your choice, on the Setup screen. This is the most common reason a page you expected never appears</td></tr>
<tr><td>You marked it excluded</td><td>Your choice</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is a WooCommerce system page</td><td>Cart, checkout and account pages are never useful link destinations</td></tr>
<tr><td>It is in a different language</td><td>An English page is never offered a Hindi destination, on any multilingual setup</td></tr>
<tr><td>It shares fewer than two meaningful words with the page being read</td><td>One word in common is coincidence, not a relationship</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>Worth reading twice.</strong> A page can be an obvious match by any human judgement and still never appear, because of one of the rules above. In practice the usual culprit is the third: the page's content type was never ticked as a link target. Nothing about the wording or the subject can rescue it.</div>

<h4>What is actually compared</h4>

<p>Everything still standing is then compared on <strong>vocabulary</strong>, and only on vocabulary. Not on categories, not on tags, not on dates, not on how the pages are linked today. Just the words.</p>

<p>And not all words. Before anything is compared, these are set aside entirely:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Ignored</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>Filler words such as "the", "and", "with"</td><td>Present everywhere, so they tell you nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Anything shorter than three letters</td><td>Same reason</td></tr>
<tr><td>Any word appearing on more than 40 percent of your pages</td><td>If most of your site uses a word, it cannot distinguish two pages. On a cooking site, "recipe" is ignored automatically</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headings, and the page title</td><td>Removed when the page is first read, so they never enter the comparison</td></tr>
</table>

<p>What is left is a page's distinctive vocabulary. The page being read is represented by its 40 most used surviving words; every other page by up to 300 of its own.</p>

<h4>How "closest" is decided</h4>

<pre class="formula">For each word the two pages share:

    how rare that word is across your whole site
      x  the smaller of the two pages' usage counts

Add all of those up, then divide by the reading page's own total.

The result is between 0 and 1: the share of this page's distinctive
vocabulary that the other page also covers.</pre>

<p>Two details in there are doing real work. <strong>Rarity</strong> means a shared unusual word counts for far more than a shared ordinary one, which is what stops every page on your site looking related to every other. <strong>The smaller of the two counts</strong> stops a page that repeats one word two hundred times from appearing related to everything that mentions it once.</p>

<p>Every eligible page gets a score this way. They are sorted, highest first, and the best of them become the shortlist. Nothing else about them is considered.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>So, in one sentence:</strong> the shortlist is the pages that share the most distinctive vocabulary with the page being read, out of everything published, in the same language, of a content type you chose as a link target.</div>

<h3>How long is the shortlist?</h3>

<p>You decide. The setting is <strong>"Potential Destination Pages shown to AI"</strong> on the Setup &amp; Dashboard screen, it starts at 15, and it accepts anything from 5 to 200. Fifteen is a starting point, not a law, so it is worth knowing what pulls in each direction.</p>

<p><strong>Why not fewer.</strong> The model can only choose from what it is shown. If the right destination is not on the list, no amount of intelligence will find it, and a short list makes that more likely on a large site. Fifteen is also comfortably more than can be used: a page accepts at most 8 new suggestions from one scan, so the model is already offered nearly twice what it could possibly need.</p>

<p><strong>Why not more.</strong> Two reasons. Cost, which is straightforward — a longer list is more to send on every page of every scan. And quality, which is less obvious: a page ranked fiftieth by shared wording is down there precisely because it has little in common with yours, so a longer list is mostly filled with weaker options. Long lists also make any model lean towards whatever sits near the top rather than judging the whole list evenly.</p>

<p><strong>A practical steer.</strong> Raise it if the AI keeps proposing nothing on pages where you can see an obvious destination it never offered. Lower it if the suggestions you reject are mostly loose, only-vaguely-related pairings.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>Something you do not have to think about.</strong> The list is filtered after it is worked out: the page itself is removed, so is anything it already links to, and so is any pair you have already approved or rejected. So the plugin always fetches noticeably more than it will show, and that headroom scales with whatever number you choose. Earlier versions fetched a flat three spare, which ran out on well-linked pages and quietly showed the model a shorter list than intended — the pages with the most established linking, where good suggestions are hardest to find, were getting the least to work with. That is fixed.</div>

<h3>What the model gets back, and what it must return</h3>

<pre class="formula">SHOWN TO THE MODEL                        THE MODEL REPLIES

Article title: Build a Content Calendar     Page number: 1
Article text:  ...your content marketing    Wording:     content marketing strategy
strategy stays consistent...                Reason:      the article plans a strategy
                                            Confidence:  0.86
Possible destinations (title - what that page covers):
  1. Content Marketing Strategy: A Guide - A content strategy sets out
     who you are writing for and why. Pillar pages anchor each theme and
     the calendar sets the publishing cadence.
  2. Social Media Scheduling Tools - Scheduling tools queue posts across
     networks from one place. Most report back on engagement per network.
  3. Editorial Workflow for Small Teams - A brief travels with each piece
     from commission to publication, naming the reviewer and the deadline.</pre>

<p>Which of the three a destination gets is <strong>"How each destination page is described"</strong> on the Setup &amp; Dashboard screen: a short summary (the default), a list of key words, or the title alone. How much of your own page is read is <strong>"Words per page sent to the AI"</strong> on the same screen.</p>

<p><strong>The summary after each title is what makes this work.</strong> A title on its own is a thin description of a page. Two posts both called "Getting started" look identical to the model, and a title that says little about its subject makes that page effectively invisible.</p>

<p>The summary is <strong>taken from the page, not written for it</strong>. Nothing is generated and no second AI request is made. The plugin scores every sentence on the page against that page's own distinctive vocabulary, which it already worked out while indexing, and keeps the two or three that represent it best.</p>

<p>One consequence is worth knowing, because it explains why the summaries are usable at all: <strong>wording your whole site repeats can never appear in one</strong>. Words used across the site are set aside before scoring, so a sentence made of them scores zero however many times it appears. Navigation text, standard introductions and "in this article you will learn" blocks are ruled out automatically, not by a list of things to avoid.</p>

<p>Sentences that say the same thing twice are dropped too, including when the page says it in different words. Pages often restate a point in a summary panel, and the restatement scores just as well as the original, so both would otherwise be chosen.</p>

<p><strong>Notice what is missing: there are no web addresses anywhere.</strong> The model is shown titles and summaries, and replies with a number. The plugin then looks up which page number 1 is and takes the address from its own records. The model never handles a web address, so it cannot invent one.</p>

<h3>What the plugin does with that reply</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Is the page number one of the ones actually shown?</td><td>Thrown away. A number outside the list means nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Look up that page. Its address comes from the plugin's own records</td><td>Cannot fail. The model had no say in the address</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Is it a different page from the one being read, and not already used?</td><td>Thrown away</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Search your article for the wording it quoted, whole words, ignoring capitals</td><td>Thrown away. <strong>This is the step that kills invented wording</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Keep the wording <em>exactly as your article writes it</em>, plus the sentence around it</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Use the model's confidence as the relevance score, kept between 0 and 1</td><td>0.7 assumed if it gives none</td></tr>
</table>

<pre class="formula">What gets saved:

Destination   page number 1  ->  the guide
Address       from the plugin's records, never from the model
Wording       "Content Marketing Strategy"
              Your capitals, because that is how your article writes it.
              The model typed it in lower case; your version wins.
Relevance     0.86</pre>

<p><strong>Why it is built this way.</strong> A model asked to produce a web address will eventually produce a believable one that does not exist. A model asked to produce wording will eventually tidy up your phrasing. Neither can happen here: the address is never in its hands, and the wording is looked up in your own article before anything is saved. Its job is deliberately narrow, choose a number and quote a phrase, and both answers are checked against things the plugin already knows.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>The one limitation worth knowing.</strong> The model reads the beginning of a page, not all of it. At the default of 1,000 words, a 3,000 word article is about one third visible, so this engine suggests links from the opening section. Increase "Words per page sent to the AI" on the Setup screen to widen that. The other two engines always read the whole page.</div>

<h3>What one page costs</h3>

<pre class="formula">Per page, at the default setting:

   about 1,300 tokens for your text
   about   750 tokens for the list of titles and instructions
   up to    800 tokens for the reply
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   roughly 2,900 tokens, and exactly one request

A 300 page site is therefore around 900,000 tokens per full scan.
Doubling the words setting roughly doubles that.</pre>

<h2>The GSC keyword engine</h2>

<p class="sub">Free. Needs Google Search Console connected. Runs first of the three.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>Neither half of this link is chosen by the plugin.</strong> Google chooses the destination, because Search Console already knows which of your pages answers a given search. The people searching choose the wording, because it is the phrase they typed. The plugin's only job is to notice where one of your pages says that phrase without linking.</div>

<h3>What Search Console provides</h3>

<pre class="formula">Search phrase   content marketing strategy
Your page       /guides/content-marketing-strategy/
Seen by         1,200 people
Your position   7th</pre>

<h3>Step 1: how much is this search phrase worth?</h3>

<p>Not every phrase is worth spending a link on. A phrase you already rank first for has nothing to gain. A phrase you rank 80th for is out of reach. The useful ones sit in between.</p>

<pre class="formula">Possible gain   how much more often people would click if this
                page reached the top 3, instead of where it sits now.
                Never less than zero.

Realistic?      full value if you already rank 20th or better.
                Reduced the further past 20th you are, because one
                internal link will not lift a page from 50th to 3rd.

Worth           how many people saw it  x  possible gain  x  realistic</pre>

<table>
<tr><th>You currently rank</th><th>1st</th><th>3rd</th><th>5th</th><th>10th</th><th>20th</th><th>40th</th><th>99th</th></tr>
<tr><td>Worth, per 1,000 people who saw it</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>50</td><td>85</td><td>102</td><td>53</td><td>21</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Reading that table:</strong> ranking 1st scores zero, because you have already won. Ranking 20th scores highest, because you are one nudge from the top of page one. Ranking 99th scores low, because no single internal link will fix that.</p>

<h3>Step 2: turn that into a relevance score</h3>

<pre class="formula">Relevance   starts at                                     0.50
            add 0.20 if you rank between 5th and 20th
            add up to 0.28, depending on how this phrase
              compares to the best phrase you have
            never more than                                 0.98</pre>

<p>The starting point of 0.50 is deliberate. A phrase people genuinely search for is stronger evidence than any amount of word similarity, so these suggestions begin above what the other engines normally reach.</p>

<h3>How the link is actually chosen</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>If it fails</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Match the address Google reports to one of your pages</td><td>The row is dropped. A page the plugin cannot identify is unusable</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><strong>The destination is now settled</strong>, chosen by Google, not by any scoring here</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><strong>The wording is now settled</strong>: the search phrase itself</td><td>Cannot fail</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Is the phrase between 2 and 4 words?</td><td>Skipped. One word is not descriptive, nine words is a sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Does the page being read actually contain that phrase?</td><td>Skipped. Nothing to turn into a link</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Does it already link there?</td><td>Skipped. Then it is not a missed link</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Would this be the fourth identical wording pointing at the same page?</td><td>Skipped, because repeating the same wording looks manipulative to Google</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Only the 500 most valuable phrases are considered per scan, and each destination gets at most one keyword suggestion per page, so the best phrase wins.</p>

<h2>Who decides what, across all three</h2>

<p>Set side by side, the three engines work quite differently. This is the clearest single summary of the whole system:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>Chooses the destination</th><th>Chooses the wording</th><th>In what order</th></tr>
<tr><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>The model, by picking a number from a shortlist someone else built</td><td>The model quotes a phrase, your page supplies the exact text</td><td>Both at once</td></tr>
<tr><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Google</td><td>The people searching</td><td>The destination arrives attached to the phrase</td></tr>
<tr><td>Related Content</td><td>Word comparison on your own site</td><td>The destination page's own title</td><td>Destination first, wording worked out afterwards</td></tr>
</table>

<p>One rule closes every path: <strong>the wording must already exist on your page</strong>, matched as whole words, ignoring capitals. If it is not there, no suggestion is made. That check happens after the engine has had its say, never before, which is why the plugin can use an AI model without ever letting it write into your content.</p>

<p>It also means the saved wording is always <em>yours</em>. Whatever the model typed, or however Google reported the phrase, what gets saved is the wording exactly as your article writes it.</p>

<h2>The two scores on every suggestion</h2>

<p>Every suggestion carries two numbers, and they answer different questions. They are worked out separately and on purpose: if one were partly made of the other, the blend at the bottom would not mean what it says.</p>

<h3>Relevance: should these two pages be connected at all?</h3>

<p>A number from 0 to 1. It says nothing about how the link reads &mdash; only whether the two pages belong together. Each engine has its own evidence, so each works it out differently:</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Engine</th><th>How relevance is decided</th><th>What a high score means</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>GSC keyword</strong></td><td>Starts at 0.50 for every match, then rises with how much traffic the phrase could realistically gain</td><td>People are searching this phrase, another of your pages already ranks for it, and this page mentions it without linking</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>AI Suggestion</strong></td><td>The model states its own confidence in the pick, from 0 to 1. If it gives none, 0.70 is assumed</td><td>The model, having read your page and a short description of each candidate, is sure this is the right destination</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Related Content</strong></td><td>The share of this page&rsquo;s distinctive vocabulary that the other page also uses</td><td>The two pages talk about the same uncommon things, not merely the same common ones</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>The three numbers are not comparable with each other.</strong> A 0.85 from the AI is the model&rsquo;s opinion. A 0.85 from Related Content would mean two pages share most of their distinctive wording, which almost never happens &mdash; that engine scores between 0.08 and 0.50 in normal use. Judge each engine against others of its own kind, which is why the review screen labels the source of every suggestion.</div>

<p><strong>Why the floor is set where it is.</strong> The Setup screen has a minimum relevance, and it applies to the Related Content engine only. Below it, two pages share so little wording that the overlap is chance. The default is deliberately low, because a much stricter test runs afterwards regardless: the wording has to be found in your page, and that rejects far more pairs than the score ever does.</p>

<h3>Naturalness: does this wording read well as a link?</h3>

<p>This looks only at the phrase that would become clickable. It knows nothing about relevance.</p>

<pre class="formula">Naturalness   start at                                    0.55
              add 0.20  if the wording is 2 to 4 words
              take off 0.15  if it is 5 words or more
              add 0.15  if it is 8 to 60 characters long
              take off 0.30  if it is under 5 characters
              take off 0.25  if it is over 70 characters

Overall       0.60 x relevance   +   0.40 x naturalness</pre>

<p>A single word is neither rewarded nor punished. Chinese, Japanese and Thai do not put spaces between words, so every phrase in those languages would otherwise count as one word and every suggestion on such a site would be marked down for the writing system it uses. Length is counted in characters rather than bytes for the same reason: a three-character Hindi or Arabic phrase is nine bytes, and counting bytes would call it long enough when it is not.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>What the percentage in the review screen is.</strong> The two blended, 60 percent relevance and 40 percent naturalness. Because well-formed wording tends to score alike, most of the movement between suggestions comes from relevance &mdash; which is the intended behaviour, not a quirk: the wording being acceptable is a condition, while the pages belonging together is the actual judgement.</div>

<h2>The Related Content engine</h2>

<p class="sub">Free, always available, no key, no internet connection. Runs last, and fills whatever the other two leave.</p>

<p>This is the engine that runs on every page no matter what. It also builds the shortlist the AI reads, so it is working even when the AI is switched on.</p>

<h3>Step 1: work out which words actually mean something</h3>

<p>Each page is reduced to the words it uses most, with filler words like "the" and "with" removed: up to 300 for a page being considered as a destination, and the top 40 for the page currently being read. Then every word is scored on how rare it is across your whole site.</p>

<pre class="formula">A word's importance depends on how few of your pages use it.

  Used on 2 of your 350 pages    ->  very important
  Used on 60 of your 350 pages   ->  mildly important
  Used on 200 of your 350 pages  ->  ignored completely

Anything appearing on more than 40 percent of your pages
is thrown out entirely.</pre>

<p><strong>Why.</strong> If a word appears on nearly every page of your site, it tells you nothing about which two pages belong together. A word that appears on only a handful tells you a great deal.</p>

<h3>Step 2: compare two pages</h3>

<pre class="formula">Take the 40 words this page uses most.

For each word the other page also uses, add up:
      that word's importance
      x  the smaller of the two pages' usage counts

Then divide by this page's own total, giving a score from 0 to 1.

At least 2 meaningful words must be shared, or the pair is ignored.</pre>

<p><strong>In plain terms:</strong> the score is the share of this page's meaningful vocabulary that the other page also covers. Taking the <em>smaller</em> of the two counts matters, because it stops a page that repeats one word two hundred times from looking related to everything on your site.</p>

<h3>How the link is actually chosen</h3>

<p>This engine works in the opposite order to the other two. It settles the destination first, then tries to work out the wording, and gives up on the pair if it cannot.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Score every other page and sort them, best first</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Take the best one left, skipping the page itself and anything already linked, suggested, or rejected before</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Ignore anything scoring below 0.08. Below that it is coincidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Take that page's <strong>title</strong>, cut it at any punctuation, and build every word group from 4 words down to 2</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Throw away groups starting or ending on a filler word, containing a bare number, or containing words like "part" or "chapter"</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Try the longest remaining group first. The first one that appears in your page becomes the wording</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td><strong>If none of them appears, give up on this pair entirely</strong> and try the next page, however high the score was</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Why longest first.</strong> "content marketing strategy" tells a reader where the link goes. "strategy" does not. Trying longer groups first means the most descriptive available wording always wins.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>This is the most common reason a link you expected never appears.</strong> Step 7. Two pages can be obviously about the same subject and still produce nothing, simply because your text never happens to contain a phrase from the other page's title. The other two engines are not restricted this way, which is much of why they run first.</div>

<div class="key"><strong>Nothing here knows your subject.</strong> The vocabulary is whatever your own pages contain, and the importance scores are worked out from your own site each time you scan. The identical method produces recipe links on a recipe site and legal links on a law firm's site, with nothing to configure.</div>

<h2>The limits every suggestion must pass</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Limit</th><th>Default</th><th>Why it exists</th></tr>
<tr><td>Links per 1,000 words</td><td>5</td><td>Stops a page turning into a wall of links</td></tr>
<tr><td>New suggestions per page</td><td>8</td><td>Keeps a review session manageable. Settings screen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wording length</td><td>2 to 4 words</td><td>Descriptive, but never a whole sentence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Same wording to the same page</td><td>3</td><td>Repetition looks manipulative to a search engine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lowest relevance accepted</td><td>0.08</td><td>Below this, shared words are coincidence</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>A worked example, from start to finish</h2>

<p>Your page mentions "content marketing strategy". Another page is titled "Content Marketing Strategy: A Complete Guide".</p>

<pre class="formula">1. The title is cut at the colon, leaving "Content Marketing Strategy".

2. Those three words are found in your page, so they can be the wording.

3. Say the word comparison scored           relevance   = 0.42

4. Naturalness  start                                   = 0.45
                add 0.30 x 0.42                         = 0.576
                add 0.15, more than one word            = 0.726
                add 0.10, 26 characters long            = 0.826

5. Overall      (0.60 x 0.42) + (0.40 x 0.826)          = 0.58</pre>

<p>It arrives in your review screen showing relevance 0.42, naturalness 0.83 and 0.58 overall, with the surrounding sentence quoted so you can judge whether it reads well before approving it.</p>

<h2>What it will never do</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Never</th><th>Why that matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Add a link without your approval</td><td>Everything waits in a review screen. Nothing reaches your content until you click</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invent wording</td><td>The words must already be on your page. If they are not, no suggestion is made at all</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link inside a heading</td><td>Headings and titles are removed before the plugin reads anything</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link across languages</td><td>An English page is never linked to a Hindi one</td></tr>
<tr><td>Send your content anywhere without a key</td><td>With no API key set up, the plugin makes no outside connections at all</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="footer">
AI Internal Linking, version 0.20.0. Every number in this document is taken from the plugin itself and checked against it. The companion document "AI Internal Linking, Overview" covers what the plugin does, how it reads your site, and how applying and undoing links works.<br />
Made by Shikher Goyal.
</div>

